      "level": "nudge",
      "title": "🪜 Try a simpler one",
      "text": "Warm up with $-30^\\circ$: adding one turn gives $-30 + 360 = 330^\\circ$, just below the positive $x$-axis in Quadrant IV. Add turns the same way here, but you will need two of them."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "$-400 + 360 = -40$, still negative. Add another $360$."
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "$-40 + 360 = 320^\\circ$, and $270^\\circ < 320^\\circ < 360^\\circ$, so it lands in Quadrant IV."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "Add full turns until the angle is positive: $-400 + 360 = -40$, then $-40 + 360 = 320$.",
    "So $-400^\\circ$ is coterminal with $320^\\circ$.",
    "Since $320^\\circ$ falls between $270^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ$, the terminal side is in Quadrant IV."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "Spin it clockwise",
      "steps": [
        "Turning $-360^\\circ$ is one full clockwise lap that puts you back at the start.",
        "That leaves $40^\\circ$ more of clockwise turn, which dips just below the positive $x$-axis.",
        "Just below the positive $x$-axis is Quadrant IV."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "⚠️ The trap here",
      "steps": [
        "The trap answer is Quadrant I — dropping the minus sign, reducing $400^\\circ$ to $40^\\circ$, and stopping there. But $-400^\\circ$ turns the other way, so it cannot land where $+40^\\circ$ does.",
        "Handle the sign first: for a negative angle, ADD $360^\\circ$ (as many times as needed) instead of subtracting. That gives $320^\\circ$, and Quadrant IV."
      ]
    }
  ],
